(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-hemostasis-quiz-version-5"]={"title":"Hemostasis — Quiz 4 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Platelets are anucleate discs roughly what size, released by fragmentation of megakaryocytes?","choices":["15–20 micrometers in diameter","7–8 micrometers in diameter","1–4 micrometers in diameter","50 micrometers in diameter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Platelets are small, 1–4 micrometer discs formed by the fragmentation of megakaryocyte cytoplasm in the bone marrow. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 31"},{"q":"Physiologic extramedullary hematopoiesis (i.e., not disease-driven) occurs during normal embryonic and fetal development, first in the yolk sac and then in the...","choices":["liver and spleen","kidney and adrenal gland","thymus and pancreas","skin and cartilage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Physiologic extramedullary hematopoiesis progresses from the yolk sac in early embryonic development to the liver and spleen during fetal development, before the bone marrow ultimately becomes the dominant site. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 12"},{"q":"Formation of the fibrin clot proceeds as fibrinogen loses four small peptides to become fibrin monomers, which then...","choices":["require Factor XIII to be present before any polymerization can even begin at all","are actively transported into platelets for long-term intracellular storage","spontaneously polymerize in the presence of calcium ions to form fibrin fibers","immediately dissolve back into fibrinogen unless plasmin is present"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Once four small peptides are cleaved from fibrinogen, the resulting fibrin monomers spontaneously polymerize — a process requiring calcium ions — to build the fibrin fiber meshwork of the clot. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 40"},{"q":"Although heparin is well known as an anticoagulant drug, endogenous (naturally occurring) heparin's blood levels are generally too low to significantly affect clotting, except locally in tissues where mast cells and basophils are abundant, such as the...","choices":["skin and hair follicles","retina and cornea","bone and cartilage","liver and lung"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Endogenous heparin's circulating levels are typically too low to have a major systemic anticoagulant effect, except in tissues like the liver and lung, where mast cells and basophils (both heparin-containing cell types) are abundant in the pericapillary connective tissue. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 50"},{"q":"Beyond their role in clot retraction, platelets are capable of protein synthesis because they retain...","choices":["a full nucleus with intact DNA","residual endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi apparatus","ribosomal RNA imported fresh from megakaryocytes after release","mitochondrial DNA used to transcribe new receptor proteins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Despite lacking a nucleus, platelets retain a residual endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi apparatus, giving them some capacity to synthesize proteins even though they cannot reproduce. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 32"},{"q":"In vitro, siliconized glass or plastic containers are used to collect blood specimens because a smooth, siliconized surface...","choices":["has no measurable effect on clotting time or platelet activation whatsoever","actively dissolves any fibrin clot that manages to form within the container","chemically binds and removes all free calcium ions from the blood sample","prevents contact activation of platelets and Factor XII, delaying clotting by about an hour"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A siliconized container surface prevents the contact activation of platelets and Factor XII that normally initiates the intrinsic pathway on a rough (e.g., glass) surface, delaying clotting by roughly an hour. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 65"},{"q":"Fibrin fibers not only bind the majority of generated thrombin but, together with antithrombin III, help prevent excessive clot growth by...","choices":["actively promoting additional fibrinogen-to-fibrin conversion at distant vascular sites","localizing and neutralizing thrombin so it cannot diffuse widely and trigger clotting elsewhere","converting thrombin back into completely inactive prothrombin molecules","stimulating widespread additional Factor XII activation throughout circulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fibrin binds most of the generated thrombin, physically localizing it to the clot, while antithrombin III neutralizes any thrombin that escapes into the general circulation — together confining coagulation to the site of injury rather than letting it spread. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 49"},{"q":"During fibrinolysis, plasmin's proteolytic action is not limited to fibrin alone — it also digests...","choices":["intact vascular endothelium directly","only newly synthesized fibrinogen, sparing existing fibrin","several other clotting factors","red blood cell membranes exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In addition to digesting fibrin fibers to dissolve the clot, plasmin also digests several other clotting factors, which can contribute to the clot's disassembly and to re-opening of the vessel. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 51"},{"q":"Iron is a functionally important component not only of hemoglobin and myoglobin but also of several enzymes, including...","choices":["digestive amylase, lipase, and trypsin enzymes","DNA polymerase and RNA polymerase enzymes","cytochromes, cytochrome oxidase, peroxidase, and catalase","renin, angiotensinogen, and aldosterone synthase enzymes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beyond hemoglobin and myoglobin, iron is a key component of several enzymes — cytochromes, cytochrome oxidase, peroxidase, and catalase — reflecting its broader role in cellular oxidative metabolism, not just oxygen transport/storage. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 24"},{"q":"Depending on the size of the injured vessel, vascular spasm (vasoconstriction) can persist for approximately...","choices":["it does not resolve until the vessel is surgically repaired","several weeks","only a few milliseconds","minutes to hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vascular spasm following vessel injury can last anywhere from minutes to hours, providing a window during which the platelet plug and coagulation cascade can further stabilize hemostasis. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 33"},{"q":"Calcium ions are required at multiple steps of the coagulation cascade, which is precisely why laboratory anticoagulants such as citrate, EDTA, and oxalate work by...","choices":["directly neutralizing circulating tissue factor","chelating (binding) calcium ions so the cascade cannot proceed","blocking thrombin receptors located on circulating platelets","directly digesting circulating fibrinogen molecules"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because calcium is required at several steps of coagulation (including fibrin monomer polymerization), agents like citrate, EDTA, and oxalate prevent clotting in collected blood samples by chelating and removing free calcium. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 65"},{"q":"The intrinsic and extrinsic pathways of coagulation ultimately converge on a shared common pathway, which begins with the activation of...","choices":["Factor XII","fibrinogen","Factor X","Factor VII"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways converge on activation of Factor X, which then combines with Factor V, calcium, and phospholipids to form the prothrombin activator complex that converts prothrombin to thrombin. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 41"},{"q":"Thrombin's positive-feedback promotion of clot extension works by thrombin acting on upstream clotting factors to...","choices":["directly stimulate additional erythropoietin release from the kidney","convert plasmin back into inactive plasminogen within the plasma","inhibit further activation of both Factor V and Factor VIII, the upstream cascade cofactors","accelerate the formation of more prothrombin activator, generating even more thrombin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thrombin has a positive-feedback action, activating Factors V and VIII (cofactors upstream in the cascade), which accelerates further formation of prothrombin activator — generating still more thrombin and rapidly amplifying clot formation once initiated. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 41"},{"q":"Intact, healthy vascular endothelium normally helps prevent unwanted clot formation in several ways, including presenting a smooth surface and secreting substances such as prostacyclin, which act to...","choices":["convert plasminogen into plasmin directly","directly cross-link fibrin fibers","inhibit platelet aggregation","promote platelet aggregation to maintain baseline clotting readiness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prostacyclin, released by healthy endothelium, inhibits platelet aggregation — one of several endothelial mechanisms (alongside a smooth non-thrombogenic surface and surface-bound heparin-like molecules) that normally keep blood fluid within intact vessels. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 46"},{"q":"The extrinsic pathway of coagulation is triggered when tissue factor, exposed by damaged tissue, forms a complex with...","choices":["Factor VII and calcium","Factor XII alone, without calcium","Factor XIII and platelets","fibrinogen directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tissue factor released from damaged tissue combines with Factor VII (and calcium) to form a complex that activates Factor X, launching the extrinsic pathway — a faster route to clot formation than the intrinsic pathway's contact-activation cascade. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 43"},{"q":"Iron bound to circulating transferrin represents only a small fraction (about 0.1%) of total body iron, consistent with the fact that transferrin's role is primarily...","choices":["long-term storage of the majority of the body's total iron reserves","transport, delivering iron to tissue cells rather than storing it","catalyzing hemoglobin synthesis directly within red cell precursors","binding oxygen directly for delivery to peripheral tissues"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because transferrin exists to shuttle iron through the plasma to wherever it's needed, only a small fraction of total body iron (about 0.1%) is found bound to it at any given moment — the much larger stores are in hemoglobin, ferritin, and myoglobin. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 25"},{"q":"ADP and thromboxane A2, secreted by activated platelets, promote further platelet recruitment through what kind of regulatory loop?","choices":["Positive feedback — they activate additional nearby platelets","Negative feedback — they suppress further platelet activation","Negative feedback that terminates the platelet plug once formed","No feedback relationship — platelet activation is purely random"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. ADP and thromboxane A2 released by already-activated platelets act on nearby platelets to activate them as well, a positive-feedback loop that rapidly recruits and aggregates more platelets to build the platelet plug. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 34"},{"q":"Hematopoiesis is regulated by two general classes of signaling molecules: growth inducers (such as interleukin-3) and...","choices":["exclusively erythropoietin, with no other regulatory molecules involved","differentiation inducers","exclusively thrombopoietin, with no other regulatory molecules involved","only inhibitory cytokines, with no positive regulatory signals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hematopoietic regulation involves growth inducers (e.g., interleukin-3), which promote proliferation of hematopoietic cells, and differentiation inducers, which direct those cells toward specific mature blood cell lineages. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 9"},{"q":"The complex that forms prothrombin activator converts prothrombin to thrombin specifically in the presence of...","choices":["potassium ions (K+)","calcium ions (Ca++)","sodium ions (Na+)","magnesium ions (Mg++) exclusively, without calcium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prothrombin activator — a complex of activated clotting factors — converts prothrombin to thrombin specifically in the presence of calcium ions, consistent with calcium's requirement at multiple steps of the coagulation cascade. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 40"},{"q":"Formation of prothrombin activator is considered the rate-limiting step of the entire coagulation cascade because...","choices":["it occurs only after fibrin has already formed and the clot is fully stabilized","it requires no calcium or other cofactors, unlike the cascade's other steps","it is actually the single fastest step, occurring before any other coagulation event at all","it is the slowest, most tightly regulated step that must occur before thrombin (and then fibrin) can form"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Because prothrombin activator formation requires the convergence and activation of multiple upstream factors, it proceeds more slowly and is more tightly regulated than the steps that follow, making it the rate-limiting step that ultimately paces how quickly thrombin and fibrin can form. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 40"},{"q":"Platelet-activating factor (PAF) is one of several named signaling molecules (alongside ADP, thromboxane A2, and von Willebrand factor) involved in the process of...","choices":["regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate secretion levels","regulation of renal erythropoietin production","regulation of hepatic bile acid synthesis pathways","platelet recruitment, adhesion, activation, and degranulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. PAF, ADP, thromboxane A2 (TXA2), and von Willebrand factor (vWF) are all named components of the coordinated process of platelet recruitment, adhesion, activation, and degranulation that builds the platelet plug at an injury site. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 35"},{"q":"Thromboxane A2, released from activated platelets, is described as especially important specifically for constricting...","choices":["smaller blood vessels","the venae cavae exclusively","only pulmonary veins","only the aorta and other large elastic arteries"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. While myogenic contraction is the dominant vasoconstrictive factor for large vessels, thromboxane A2 released from platelets is highlighted as especially important for constriction of smaller blood vessels. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 33"},{"q":"Because iron is a component of myoglobin as well as hemoglobin, myoglobin accounts for approximately what fraction of total body iron?","choices":["About 4%","About 50%","About 0.001%","About 20%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Myoglobin, the oxygen-storing pigment of muscle, accounts for roughly 4% of total body iron — a much smaller share than hemoglobin's approximately 65%, but still a physiologically meaningful reserve. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 24"},{"q":"Local autacoid factors contributing to vascular spasm are released from three sources: traumatized tissue, vascular endothelium, and...","choices":["hepatocytes","red blood cells","platelets","renal tubular cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Local autacoid factors promoting vascular spasm are released from traumatized tissue, the vascular endothelium itself, and platelets, together reinforcing vasoconstriction alongside the myogenic and nervous-reflex components. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 33"},{"q":"All blood cell lineages — red cells, white cells, and platelets — ultimately derive from a common ancestor described as...","choices":["a pluripotent hematopoietic stem cell","hepatocytes that migrate to the bone marrow","mature platelets that dedifferentiate as needed","mature erythrocytes that later transform into other lineages"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A single pluripotent hematopoietic stem cell population in the bone marrow gives rise to all blood cell lineages, which then differentiate along separate pathways under the influence of growth and differentiation inducers. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 9"},{"q":"Antithrombin III limits coagulation chiefly by...","choices":["activating Factor XII directly to initiate the intrinsic coagulation pathway","stimulating additional platelet aggregation at the injury site","directly neutralizing thrombin and several other activated clotting factors","converting fibrin strands back into soluble fibrinogen molecules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antithrombin III is a plasma protein that directly binds and neutralizes thrombin, along with several other activated clotting factors, helping to confine and eventually shut down the coagulation process — an effect greatly accelerated when it is bound to heparin. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 46"},{"q":"A very low platelet count (thrombocytopenia) tends to cause spontaneous bleeding chiefly because platelets are normally needed to...","choices":["carry oxygen directly to peripheral tissue beds throughout the body","produce erythropoietin in the kidney in response to tissue hypoxia","produce clotting factors that are synthesized nowhere else in the entire body","plug small breaks in capillaries and small vessels that occur during normal daily activity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Under normal conditions, small vessel breaks occur routinely during everyday activity; platelets continuously plug these minor breaks. Without adequate platelets, these small leaks are not sealed, resulting in spontaneous bleeding, often visible as small hemorrhagic spots. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 31"},{"q":"The intrinsic pathway of coagulation is triggered when blood makes contact with a negatively charged surface, such as exposed collagen, activating...","choices":["tissue factor","Factor II (prothrombin)","fibrinogen","Factor XII"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Contact of blood with a negatively charged surface (like exposed collagen) triggers activation of Factor XII, initiating the intrinsic pathway's sequential activation of Factors XI, IX, and eventually X. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 42"},{"q":"Because iron loosely bound to transferrin can be released to any tissue cell that needs it, this loose (rather than tight) binding is functionally important because it...","choices":["prevents iron from ever leaving the bloodstream once it is bound","means transferrin can only ever deliver iron to the bone marrow","allows efficient, reversible delivery of iron to whichever cells require it","causes iron to become permanently inactivated the moment it binds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Transferrin's loose (rather than irreversible) binding of iron allows the iron to be readily released and taken up by whichever tissue cells currently need it, making transferrin an efficient, reusable transport vehicle rather than a permanent iron trap. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 25"},{"q":"Taken together, the three named contributors to vascular spasm (myogenic contraction, local autacoids, and sympathetic nervous reflexes) serve the overall purpose of...","choices":["increasing blood flow to the injury site to speed tissue healing immediately","directly forming the fibrin clot itself without any coagulation factors involved","permanently sealing the vessel without requiring any further hemostatic steps","reducing blood flow/loss at the injury site during the earliest moments after vessel damage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All three mechanisms converge on the same immediate goal: constricting the injured vessel to reduce blood flow and limit blood loss in the earliest moments after injury, buying time for the platelet plug and coagulation cascade to form. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 33"}]};
